# Answer 1 · The Status of Women's Studies in Pakistan and the Autonomy/Integration Debate (CSS 2016)
@py question 2016-2

## The answer map
::: fig mindmap | The answer map: every branch is one numbered heading of the model answer below.
@note Part one describes where the field stands; part two gives a clear view on the debate — argue for a dual strategy.
@ask ① WRITE A NOTE on the status of Women's Studies in Pakistan
@ask ② GIVE YOUR VIEWS on the autonomy/integration debate
(1) Introduction: Women's Studies puts women at the centre of study; it began in US universities in 1969–70; Pakistan founded five centres in 1989; thesis — a young field that needs both strategies
(2) Origins in Pakistan: NGOs like Shirkat Gah researched women first; WAF challenged the Hudood laws; Mumtaz and Shaheed wrote the first history
(3) Status today: five university centres set up in 1989; degrees now offered up to PhD; own journals; CSS subject since 2016
(4) Achievements: research helped pass pro-women laws; graduates staff NCSW and NGOs; shaped the 2002 women's policy
(5) Problems: depends on short donor projects; few trained teachers; Western books, little local theory; seen as a "Western agenda"
(6) The debate: framed by Bowles and Klein in 1983; autonomy means a separate department; integration means gender in every subject
(7) Case for each: autonomy gives depth and a protected voice; integration reaches every student and changes subjects
(8) Risks of each: autonomy risks an ignored "women's corner"; integration risks one token lecture
(9) My view: keep strong departments for experts; carry gender into law, medicine, economics; departments should drive integration
(10) Conclusion: the field has arrived but is fragile; roots and reach are both needed
:::

## Decoding the question
The question has **two parts**. **"Write a note on the status"** asks for a clear, organised picture of where the field stands today: its history, institutions, achievements and problems. **"Give your views"** asks for your own position on the autonomy/integration debate, supported by evidence. The best answers **link the two parts**: the problems you find in part one should explain the view you take in part two.

## The model answer

### 1. Introduction

#### i) Definition
**Women's Studies** is the academic field that puts **women at the centre** of study. It studies women's lives, work, history and experience, and aims to improve them.

#### ii) Where it began
It began in American universities in ==1969–70== — a course at Cornell and the first programme at San Diego State — as "**the academic arm of the women's movement**".

#### iii) Pakistan and the question
In Pakistan, the field entered the universities in ==1989==. Three decades later it is recognised and productive, but still fragile. Its future depends on combining a **separate academic base** with **gender in every discipline** — the "dual strategy".

### 2. Origins of Women's Studies in Pakistan

#### i) Research by women's organisations
Research on women began **outside the universities**. **Shirkat Gah** (==1975==) and the **Aurat Foundation** (==1986==) studied women's legal rights, work and political participation.

#### ii) The Women's Action Forum
The **Women's Action Forum** (==1981==) was formed to resist the **Hudood Ordinances** of ==1979== — laws on sexual offences that harmed women. Its members produced legal and social analysis that became the base of later scholarship.

#### iii) The first history
**Khawar Mumtaz and Farida Shaheed** wrote *Women of Pakistan: Two Steps Forward, One Step Back?* (==1987==), the country's first standard history of the women's movement. The universities followed the activists.

### 3. The status of the field today

#### i) The centres of 1989
The federal **Ministry of Women's Development** set up **Women's Studies centres** at **Quaid-i-Azam University** (Islamabad) and the Universities of **Karachi**, the **Punjab** (Lahore), **Peshawar** and **Balochistan** (Quetta). Some later became centres of excellence.

#### ii) Degrees and departments
Most centres have renamed themselves "**Gender Studies**", showing a shift from studying women alone to studying relations between women and men. Many universities now offer **BS, MPhil and PhD** degrees, and gender courses appear in sociology and social work.

#### iii) Journals and recognition
Journals such as the ***Pakistan Journal of Gender Studies*** publish local research. From ==CE-2016==, Gender Studies became a **CSS optional subject** — a sign that the field has arrived.

### 4. Achievements

#### i) Research that became law
Research on honour crimes, harassment and violence at home gave evidence for the laws on **workplace harassment** (==2010==) and **honour killing** (==2016==).

#### ii) Trained people
Graduates staff the **National Commission on the Status of Women** (==2000==), provincial commissions, women's protection centres and NGOs.

#### iii) Policy
Researchers helped shape the **National Policy for Development and Empowerment of Women** (==2002==).

#### iv) New words for hidden problems
The field gave the public **words** for problems once kept inside the home — **harassment**, **domestic violence**, **care work**. Once a harm has a name, it can be reported, measured and brought before a court.

### 5. Problems

::: table | The field's problems and their effects.
@widths 3.2,6.8
Problem | Effect
Depends on donor projects | Centres shrink when a project ends; outsiders set the agenda
Few trained teachers | Few PhDs; many teachers borrowed from other subjects
Western books, mostly in English | Little local theory; limited reach beyond elite students
Weak data | Small studies; little long-term research on Pakistani families
Social resistance | Seen as a "Western" or "anti-family" agenda
Ghettoisation | Mostly women students and staff; little influence on others
:::

Two problems matter most. **Funding** shapes everything: when a donor project ends, research teams scatter. **Resistance** is just as serious: because the field questions family roles, it is easily painted as foreign.

### 6. The autonomy/integration debate

#### i) Origin
The debate was set out by **Gloria Bowles and Renate Duelli Klein** in *Theories of Women's Studies* (==1983==), a book on the FPSC reading list. The question was simple: **where should the new knowledge live?**

#### ii) Autonomy — defined
**Autonomy** means keeping Women's Studies as a **separate department**, with its own teachers, courses, degrees and budget. *Example:* a Department of Gender Studies awarding its own PhDs.

#### iii) Integration — defined
**Integration** (or **mainstreaming**) means **adding women and gender to every existing subject**. *Example:* a law degree that teaches women's inheritance rights, or a medical degree that teaches maternal health properly.

### 7. The case for each

#### i) The case for autonomy
- **Depth:** students can study gender for a whole degree, not one lecture.
- **New theory:** new ideas need a place where they are the main business.
- **Protection:** gender topics inside other departments disappear when a friendly teacher leaves.
- **A critical voice:** an independent unit can criticise male bias elsewhere.

#### ii) The case for integration
- **Reach:** every future doctor, lawyer and civil servant meets gender.
- **Real change:** the subjects themselves are corrected, not only a separate corner.
- **Cost:** cheaper for poor universities.
- **Men involved:** gender stops being "women's business".

### 8. The risks of each

::: fig compare | The risk of each strategy.
@vs vs
## Autonomy risks ghettoisation
- A small, separate "women's corner"
- Mostly women students and staff
- Ignored by other departments
- Costly; dependent on donors
## Integration risks dilution
- Gender cut to one token lecture
- Depends on a few willing teachers
- Loss of expert knowledge
- Loss of the feminist edge
:::

In short: autonomy without integration stays in a corner; integration without autonomy has no experts to lead it. Pakistan today shows the first risk clearly: its departments are strong on paper but isolated in practice.

### 9. My view: a dual strategy for Pakistan

#### i) Why autonomy is still needed
The field is young and short of trained teachers. Pakistan needs **strong departments** to train specialists and produce local research.

#### ii) Why integration is essential
The greatest impact lies outside the department: **maternal health** in medical colleges, **family and criminal law** in law schools, **unpaid work** in economics, **gender-aware textbooks** in teacher training, and gender analysis in **civil-service academies**.

#### iii) How the two should work together
::: fig flow | The dual strategy: departments drive integration.
@per 4
Strong departments: train experts, do local research
Train other teachers: gender modules for law, medicine, economics
Integration: every professional meets gender
Feedback: new questions return to the departments
:::

#### iv) An answer to critics
Some fear that integration will be only a token lecture. That is why the **departments must lead it**, with trained teachers and approved content. Local integration also answers the "**Western agenda**" charge: gender taught inside a Pakistani law or medical course is plainly about Pakistan's own problems.

### 10. Conclusion
Women's Studies in Pakistan has moved in three decades from activist research to university degrees and real influence on law. Yet it still depends on donors, lacks teachers and sits in a corner of the university. The debate is therefore practical, not academic. The answer is **both**: well-funded departments that make gender part of every discipline — as the **Beijing Platform** (==1995==) did for governments with its "twin track" of special measures plus **gender mainstreaming**.

## Why this answer scores
- **Both parts** are answered under headings taken from the question ("status"; "views").
- The introduction gives a **simple definition**, the **origin**, the **Pakistani context** and a **thesis**.
- Each sub-heading answers one point in a short paragraph, with **key words in bold**.
- The debate is broken down properly: **origin → definitions with examples → case for each → risks → view**.
- **A table and two figures** (comparison and flow) make the answer easy to mark at a glance.
- The view is **clear and practical**, and it grows out of the problems found in part one.
